{
  "id": 1,
  "slug": "first-kite",
  "title": "First Kite",
  "concept": "What makes a subnet public, and how a request reaches an application through a load balancer.",
  "guidance": "full",
  "region": "us-east-1",
  "azs": 2,
  "narration": [
    {
      "id": "welcome",
      "text": "Welcome to the sky above N. Virginia. The dashed border is your VPC: your own region of the AWS network. The Cloudlings on the ground are the internet, and their kites are requests. Right now there is no way in. Click any Cloudling to hear what it wants.",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "acknowledged"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "instances",
      "text": "Two EC2 instances are already running in the mid band, in private subnets. They are locked for now; we will look inside them in the next level. They wear their own security group, ec2-app-sg; the load balancer you are about to add brings its own, alb-sg. Each kind of component gets its own group, so each opens only what it needs. Our job is to let the green kites reach them safely.",
      "highlight": "resource:app-a",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "acknowledged"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "place-igw",
      "text": "First, a gate. Drag the Internet Gateway from the tray down to the bottom edge of the VPC, in the band marked Low – public. When that part of the sky lights up, let go. Let go anywhere else and it comes back to the tray.",
      "highlight": "palette:igw",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "resource-exists",
          "resourceId": "igw"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "add-route",
      "text": "A gate is no use without a signpost. Click a public subnet in the low band and add a route: Internet (0.0.0.0/0) to the Internet Gateway. That route is what makes a subnet public. (0.0.0.0/0 means every address. Click the VPC's own range, 10.0.0.0/16, above its border to see how these numbers work.)",
      "highlight": "subnet:public-a",
      "pulse": ["route-table:rt-public"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "route-present",
          "routeTableId": "rt-public",
          "destination": "internet",
          "target": "internet-gateway"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "place-alb",
      "text": "Now the front door. Drag the Application Load Balancer into the low band, over the public subnets. When they light up, let go: it settles across both, one node in each Availability Zone.",
      "highlight": "palette:alb",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "resource-exists",
          "resourceId": "alb"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "open-sg",
      "text": "The load balancer wears a security group, drawn as a pencilled outline, and it starts with no inbound rules, so everything is refused. Click the load balancer and add an inbound rule: HTTPS 443 from Anywhere.",
      "highlight": "resource:alb",
      "pulse": ["security-group:sg-alb"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "security-group-allows",
          "securityGroupId": "sg-alb",
          "ports": "https",
          "peer": {
            "kind": "anywhere"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "listener",
      "text": "The green kites fly on HTTPS 443. On the same card, set the listener to HTTPS 443 so the load balancer is listening on that port.",
      "highlight": "resource:alb",
      "pulse": ["listener:alb"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "listener-is",
          "loadBalancerId": "alb",
          "listener": "https"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "targets",
      "text": "Last, tell the load balancer where to send requests. On its card, tick both EC2 instances as targets (or drag from its handle to each EC2 instance).",
      "highlight": "resource:alb",
      "pulse": ["targets:alb"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "targets-registered",
          "loadBalancerId": "alb",
          "atLeast": 2
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "fly",
      "text": "Ready. Press \"Fly kites\" and watch. Three green kites are visitors; the red one is aiming at SSH 22.",
      "highlight": "button:fly",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "level-complete"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "inspect",
      "text": "Click any Cloudling to see its kite's journey, check by check. The red kite bounced off the outline: no rule allows port 22, and a security group refuses whatever it does not allow.",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "acknowledged"
      }
    }
  ],
